### F7 — `_weighted_cluster_center` off-by-one when missing-data outliers (-3) are present [out-of-theme]
severity: high
evidence: sklearn/cluster/_hdbscan/hdbscan.py:894-895 — `n_clusters = len(set(self.labels_) - {-1, -2})`. This subtraction excludes noise and infinite-outlier labels but *not* the missing-data label -3 defined in `_OUTLIER_ENCODING["missing"]["label"]`. When any sample has NaN inputs (label -3), the resulting `n_clusters` overcounts by one; the loop `for idx in range(n_clusters):` at line 907 iterates one extra index whose `mask = self.labels_ == idx` matches no samples, so `data` is empty. `np.average(data, weights=strength, axis=0)` on empty data raises `ZeroDivisionError`.
scenario: "User fits `HDBSCAN(store_centers='centroid')` on data containing NaN rows → -3 outlier labels appear → `_weighted_cluster_center` computes an inflated n_clusters → `np.average` on the trailing empty cluster raises."
contract: Change the exclusion to `set(self.labels_) - {-1, -2, -3}` (or use `set(self.labels_) - OUTLIER_SET`) so all reserved negative outlier labels are excluded.
instances: single-instance

